   "body": "### Summary\n\nWhen two installed distributions claim the **same file path**, uv resolves the conflict silently, and the resulting state cannot be repaired with uv commands. The underlying packaging overlap is an upstream (aws-cdk) defect and I've reported it there separately \u2014 this issue is about the installer behaviour it exposes, which seems worth handling regardless of who created the overlap.\n\nThree behaviours, in increasing order of surprise:\n\n1. **A path collision between two distributions is resolved silently.** Nothing warns that two wheels wrote the same file, or which one won.\n2. **The file survives uninstall of *every* distribution that claims it.** After uninstalling both, the file is still on disk \u2014 orphaned, owned by nothing.\n3. **`uv sync` neither detects nor restores a missing file.** Deleting the directory and re-running `uv sync` leaves it missing, because installed-ness is judged from dist-info rather than from files. The result is an environment that imports broken and that no uv command repairs.\n\n### Reproducer\n\nThe concrete overlap (~30 seconds, current versions):\n\n```bash\nuv init repro && cd repro\nuv add aws-cdk-lib\nuv run python -c \"\nimport collections, importlib.metadata as md\nT='aws_cdk/cloud_assembly_schema/__init__.py'\no=collections.defaultdict(list)\nfor d in md.distributions():\n    for f in (d.files or []):\n        if str(f).replace(chr(92),'/').endswith(T):\n            o[T].append((d.metadata['Name'], d.version))\nprint(o)\"\n```\n\n```\n{'aws_cdk/cloud_assembly_schema/__init__.py': [\n    ('aws-cdk.cloud-assembly-schema', '54.16.0'),\n    ('aws-cdk-lib', '2.263.0')]}\n```\n\nBoth wheels ship that path. `aws-cdk-lib` depends on `aws-cdk.cloud-assembly-schema`, so both are always present.\n\nThen, in an environment where the collision exists:\n\n```bash\nuv pip uninstall aws-cdk-lib aws-cdk-cloud-assembly-schema\nls .venv/Lib/site-packages/aws_cdk/cloud_assembly_schema/__init__.py   # still there\n```\n\n```bash\nrm -rf .venv/Lib/site-packages/aws_cdk/cloud_assembly_schema\nuv sync --locked          # reports success\nuv run python -c \"import aws_cdk\"\n# ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'aws_cdk.cloud_assembly_schema._jsii'\n```\n\n`uv sync --reinstall` does restore the files.\n\n### Why this matters beyond the one package\n\nThe two builds of that file are not equivalent \u2014 they declare different jsii type names \u2014 so *which* one wins changes program behaviour, and the losing outcome fails at runtime with an error that points nowhere near packaging (`KeyError` inside jsii's reference map). Because the collision is silent, there is nothing in the install output connecting the two.\n\nThe general shape: hash-pinning in the lockfile constrains each distribution's **bytes**, but says nothing about which distribution wins when two unpack to the same path. `--locked` does not help, since both install exactly as locked.\n\n### What would help\n\nAny one of these would have turned a multi-hour investigation into a one-line message:\n\n* Warn (or error under a flag) when an install writes a path already claimed by another distribution, naming both.\n* On uninstall, remove or report files claimed by multiple distributions rather than leaving them orphaned.\n* Have `uv sync` verify that files recorded in RECORD actually exist, rather than treating dist-info as sufficient evidence of installation.\n\n### Environment\n\n**All observations are from a single Windows machine; I have not tested Linux or macOS**, and file-collision behaviour may differ there.\n\n* uv 0.9.9 (4fac4cb7e 2025-11-12)\n* Python 3.11.14\n* Windows-10-10.0.26200-SP0\n* Packages: `aws-cdk-lib` 2.263.0 / `aws-cdk.cloud-assembly-schema` 54.16.0 in the reproducer; failure originally observed at 2.254.0 / 53.24.0\n\nI saw both winners across repeated environment creations on this machine, and later creations stopped producing the failing one. **I'm not quoting a rate** \u2014 the variation is unattributed and a single machine isn't a sample. The three behaviours above, however, reproduce deterministically.\n",
